wash (0.14.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * agent: the agent feature got a navigation layer. Every door — the
    sidebar's, a roster row, a notification — now goes TO the session that
    wants you, opening a window only when there is none, instead of stacking
    up a fresh Agent window per click. A blocked agent raises a toast naming
    its question, and clicking it lands on that session rather than on the
    app in general.
  * agent: a question waiting on you now marks its window's taskbar pill, and
    looking at the window clears it — the router owns that, so no app can
    leave a pill blinking at something you have already read.
  * agent: the sessions list is always on screen and resizable, in place of a
    toggle that opened it on three different rules. Its width is remembered
    per window.
  * agent: history search is full text. A query is words, all of them
    required, in any order; a result shows the line that matched with the
    terms marked; and a trigram index keeps it instant as history grows.
  * agent: the new-session form opens on the agent and folder you used last.
  * chrome: a menu closes when the window under it moves. Dragging a titlebar
    suppresses the mouse event menus were listening for, so they hung in
    mid-air over whatever was underneath.
  * router: a router no longer takes a control socket another router is still
    answering on. With sessions outliving their browsers, several routers at
    once is normal, and the loser's apps were reaching the wrong router and
    failing to launch at all.
  * router: a session outlives the browser that opened it, and comes back
    whole — with its transcript, its roster and its unanswered questions.
  * sidebar: the right rail became host-aware. Counts, questions and doors
    span every connected host, and the verbs moved into the apps, which can
    act on any host rather than only the local one.

 -- sirmick <sirmick@gmail.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 14:40:15 -0700

wash (0.13.2-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * term: a terminal no longer stops following its own output after a heavy
    burst. The scrollback resync reset the xterm out of band, which jumped
    xterm's asynchronous parse queue and detached the viewport — every byte
    arrived and was parsed, but the screen stayed frozen thousands of lines
    above the cursor until you scrolled. The reset now travels in band with
    the data it is ordered against.
  * agent: a finished session no longer sits on "working…" with a live Stop
    button until the next turn. The response that ends a turn can overtake
    the tail of that turn's own notification stream, and the late chunks were
    re-marking the session busy after it had already finished.
  * agent: an agent that runs a command, reads its output and releases the
    terminal in quick succession keeps that command's output in the
    transcript. Release dropped the record before the pty's exit could
    complete the transcript entry, leaving a dead live-terminal frame.
  * router: reconnect replay robustness — a truncated scrollback replay is
    cut through its first newline, so a reattached terminal no longer opens
    with a torn escape sequence rendered as garbage; a failed resync write
    now retries via the watchdog instead of leaving the terminal wiped; and
    video channels are nudged for a fresh frame rather than replayed.

 -- sirmick <sirmick@gmail.com>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 07:30:00 -0700

wash (0.13.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * remote-fs: recover from a silent SSH link death on both the mount data
    path and the shared watch channel. A NAT/conntrack drop, a suspended
    laptop, or a pulled cable now surfaces as an error and reconnects instead
    of silently wedging the mount or stalling live updates.
  * remote: the wash-remote supervisor no longer data-races its published
    host and mount state — the status slices are rebuilt copy-on-write so a
    concurrent state push can't read a half-mutated slice.
  * wash-vm: the in-browser (WASM RISC-V) VM inflates the gzip-compressed
    shell bundle in its bootstrap, so the desktop mounts again instead of
    hanging on "wash-router up" with an empty screen. Adds a headless boot
    gate (make browser-vm-test) so it can't regress unseen.

 -- sirmick <sirmick@gmail.com>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 21:33:00 -0700

wash (0.13.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * agent: wash now offers coding agents its own filesystem and terminals
    over ACP. An agent that opts in reads and writes files THROUGH wash —
    confined to the folder you started it in, through the layer the desktop
    watches — and hands wash its shell commands instead of forking them
    inside the adapter. Those commands run as real wash ptys: you watch them
    scroll in the transcript, interrupt them, and keep their output and exit
    status after they finish.
  * agent: sessions can run in the editor. wash-edit hosts an agent in the
    pane its terminals already live in, starting in the folder you have open
    — nobody is asked which folder — and a tool row naming a file opens that
    file in the buffer above the transcript.
  * agent: a per-session "yolo" mode lets wash approve tool requests without
    asking. Deliberately loud: a standing badge, every auto-approval written
    into the transcript, dead when the session ends, and unable to reverse an
    explicit deny rule.
  * terminal: closing a tab or a window now asks first, naming what is still
    running. Closing killed shells outright on one keystroke, with no undo.
  * terminal: each split pane carries its own status bar, so an unfocused
    pane's ssh/root/agent state is visible rather than implied.
  * files: a symlink to a folder behaves as a folder — it can be chosen in a
    folder picker, entered in the file tree, and sorted with the directories.
    It was previously filtered out of directory pickers entirely.
  * test: the three e2e specs that had been red on every branch for weeks are
    fixed, so `make push` gates honestly again. Two were real defects — the
    boot splash swallowing the connection banner's retry click, and a stale
    log match launching terminals before DISPLAY was published — not load.

 -- sirmick <sirmick@gmail.com>  Fri, 07 Aug 2026 14:25:27 -0700

wash (0.12.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * shell: a connection lost before the desktop finished painting no longer
    hides behind the boot splash. The splash is a full-screen opaque overlay
    torn down when the session app reports the desktop painted; if the router
    dropped first that signal never arrived, so "starting wash…" sat on top of
    the connection banner for up to 12 seconds and swallowed clicks on
    "Reconnect now" — exactly when a user needs it. The splash now stands down
    as soon as the connection is lost, and the banner outranks it.
  * router: the env.publish log line names the keys it accepted rather than
    only counting them, so "the compositor published its display" is legible
    in a log instead of being inferred.
  * test: the e2e gate gates again. The three specs that had been red on every
    branch for weeks — reconnect and the two display capstones — were real
    defects, not load, and are fixed; a fourth latent false-pass in the
    terminal burst soak (an assertion that matched the command it had just
    typed) is fixed with them. See docs/FLAKE_LOG.md 2026-08-06.

